[00:00] [scene:intro]
VISUAL: "744B PARAMETERS" on screen, with "< 1 H100 FAN" below.
AUDIO: '744 billion parameters running on a machine that costs less than one [music] cooling fan from an H100 GPU. Not a Temu knockoff either. GLM 5.2, an open model that sits above Claude Opus in the'

[00:14] [scene:intro]
VISUAL: "colibrì" logo with a pixelated bird.
AUDIO: 'latest LLM Arena benchmarks, answering correctly from a laptop with 25 GB of RAM. The model is 15 times bigger than the memory it\'s running in. [music]'

[00:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The engine pulling this off is called Colibri, 1,300 lines of C. No Python, no GPU, and the loophole it exploits is hiding inside every big AI model you\'ve used this year. By the end of this'

[00:39] [scene:concept]
VISUAL: "370 GB" displayed, referring to the model size at 4-bit precision.
AUDIO: 'video, you\'ll know exactly how a model that can\'t fit in your computer runs on it anyway, and why the number everyone\'s laughing at is the wrong thing to look at. Try and wrap your mind around it.'

[00:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; GLM 5.2\'s weights, compressed down to 4 bits per number, take up 370 GB. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; You\'d normally need half a data center of GPUs lying around. Call it $40,000 or'

[01:04] [scene:concept]
VISUAL: Diagram showing "SSD" connected to "MODEL" with a dashed arrow.
AUDIO: 'a Mac Studio with 256 gigs of unified memory. Anything less and the model refuses to load. The obvious workaround is reading the model'

[01:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'straight off the SSD instead. For a normal model, that idea is dead on arrival. Every parameter is needed for every single token, so you\'d be pulling all 370 GB off the disk for [music]'

[01:30] [scene:concept]
VISUAL: Text "GLM-5.2 IS NOT A NORMAL MODEL".
AUDIO: 'every word. On the fastest consumer SSD money can buy, that\'s almost half a minute per word. Good luck writing your code like that. So, why does Colibri work? Because GLM 5.2 is not built like'

[01:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a normal model. GLM 5.2 is a mixture of experts model. Inside each layer, instead of one giant block of neurons, there are 256'

[01:56] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Diagram of "ONE MOE LAYER" with a "ROUTER" and "256 EXPERTS" grid.
AUDIO: 'smaller ones called experts. In front of them sits a tiny [music] piece of the network called the router. Every time a token comes through, the router scores all 256 experts and picks the eight best'

[02:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'suited for that exact token at that exact layer. The other 248 stay asleep. Now, take experts with a grain of salt. People picture a math guy, a code guy, a'

[02:23] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Diagram showing the router picking 3 experts from the grid.
AUDIO: 'poetry guy, and figure you could keep the code guy and delete the rest. Experts are really unlabelled blobs of learned weights with messy, overlapping jobs, &gt;&gt; [music]'

[02:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; and the router\'s choice changes token by token, layer by layer. The same word [music] gets different experts in a different sentence. There is no coding subset to keep. Add it up, and out of 744'

[02:47] [scene:concept]
VISUAL: "40B / 744B" with a progress bar indicating "~5%" and text "DEAD WEIGHT DOESN'T NEED TO BE IN RAM".
AUDIO: 'billion parameters, only about 40 billion do any work per token, &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; around 5%. The rest of the model is, at any given moment, that weight, and that weight doesn\'t need to be in RAM. That\'s'

[02:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the concept Colibri rides on. Split the model [music] into the parts every token touches, the attention layers, the embeddings, one shared [music] expert, add up to just under 10 GB at 4-bit'

[03:11] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Diagram showing "~10 GB • PINNED" in RAM, and "21,500 EXPERTS" on disk (19 MB each, 370 GB file).
AUDIO: 'precision. Those get pinned in RAM permanently. The other 21 and 1/2 thousand experts, 19 MB a piece, sit in a 370 [music] GB file on the SSD. Then, for every'

[03:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'token, the dense core in RAM does its work, and the router names its eight experts for this layer. Colibri checks a cache [music] it keeps in the spare RAM.'

[03:35] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Animation of the router picking experts, with some experts highlighted.
AUDIO: 'Already loaded, multiply and move on. Missing, the engine reads that exact expert off the SSD at its exact [music] byte offset, multiplies, and evicts whichever expert has gone unused'

[03:49] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Animation showing experts being loaded, with a ".coli_usage" file mentioned.
AUDIO: 'longest. Repeated across all 75 layers, and [music] the next token starts the cycle over. A fully cold token touches about 11 GB of disk reads. That\'s insane,'

[04:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; but routing is cute. A small crowd of favorite experts handles most of the traffic, so Colibri keeps a usage log on disk. And on the next launch, it pins your personal hot experts into whatever'

[04:14] [scene:comparison]
VISUAL: Diagram comparing `llama.cpp`'s memory mapping (OS guessing) with an SSD.
AUDIO: 'RAM is left over. The engine measurably gets faster the more you use it. Of course, there was pushback when this released. Llama.cpp can already memory map a model bigger than your RAM. True,'

[04:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'but memory mapping is the operating system guessing. It faults pages in blind, one small read at a time, blocking the whole thread while it waits, with no idea which weights are important. Colibri knows. The moment the'

[04:41] [scene:comparison]
VISUAL: Diagram comparing `llama.cpp`'s memory mapping with Colibri's asynchronous loading of experts from SSD.
AUDIO: 'router picks its eight experts, [music] the engine requests all of them from the disk at once, asynchronously, while the CPU keeps crunching. [music] It even runs the next layer\'s router'

[04:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'early on partial information, so experts are loading before they\'re needed. A page fault can\'t see the future. A router [music] can, a little. You\'re all waiting on the catch, right? On the author\'s laptop, this runs at a'

[05:07] [scene:result]
VISUAL: "THE HARDWARE LADDER • TOK/S" with benchmarks: "0.28 PCIe 5 DRIVE" and "0.10 BUDGET SSD".
AUDIO: 'tenth of a token per second. A 500 token answer [music] takes an hour and a half. The community benchmarked up the hardware ladder. A budget SSD gets [music] you .1 tokens per second. A top'

[05:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'PCIe 5 drive, .28. A 128 gig Framework laptop, .37. And an M5 [music] Max with a monster internal SSD tops the chart at one token'

[05:33] [scene:concept]
VISUAL: Diagram showing "SSD" with "READS" arrows and a "WRITES" button.
AUDIO: 'per second. That\'s the ceiling today. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; And no, this won\'t kill your SSD. Streaming is pure reads, and reads don\'t wear out flash.'

[05:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; Writes do. The real risk is a cheap drive thermally throttling after hours of running flat out. So, given the speed, is it useless then? &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; Depends what you\'re counting. A tenth of a token per second is also 8,000 tokens'

[05:57] [scene:humor]
